WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.282
Remote responses are untrusted input.

2
00:00:02.282 --> 00:00:12.033
A status code, header set, content type, body, or error message may be
missing, stale, malformed, oversized, misleading, or inconsistent with the
expected contract.

3
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:22.337
HTTPS protects transport to the endpoint, but it does not prove that a
response is semantically correct, authorized for the current user, safe to
store, or appropriate to act on.

4
00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:25.725
The client must validate what it receives before using it.

5
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.390
Response validation should check both structure and meaning.

6
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:39.625
JSON parsing tells you whether bytes can be decoded, but schema validation
concepts help confirm expected fields, types, optional fields, unknown
fields, and nested shapes.

7
00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:44.743
The client should decide how strict to be when fields are missing or extra
fields appear.

8
00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:56.015
For high-impact workflows, it should also check identifiers, tenant
context, status transitions, timestamps, authorization context, and
business conditions before updating local state.

9
00:00:56.015 --> 00:00:58.435
Error responses need their own handling.

10
00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:06.111
A remote denial, timeout, rate limit, malformed response, partial result,
or service outage should not be interpreted as success.

11
00:01:06.111 --> 00:01:13.925
At the same time, raw remote error messages may contain sensitive details
or confusing text that should not be shown directly to users.

12
00:01:13.925 --> 00:01:24.990
Safe clients separate internal diagnostic information from user-facing
messages, preserve enough context for troubleshooting, and avoid logging
sensitive response bodies by default.

13
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.065
Non-JSON formats deserve caution.

14
00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:36.331
XML, YAML, CSV, compressed files, archives, and binary responses can
introduce parsing complexity, resource consumption, and downstream
processing risk.

15
00:01:36.331 --> 00:01:44.146
A secure client sets size limits, validates content type, rejects
unexpected formats, and uses safe parsers appropriate to the data.

16
00:01:44.146 --> 00:01:51.960
The theme is consistent: do not let a remote service silently redefine
what your application accepts, stores, displays, or acts upon.
